# Using the data
- <setting> is authored by a user and varies in detail. Names, core identity, and fixed world rules remain authoritative; relationships, locations, and other mutable facts are initial state that later transcript or profile may update. If sparse, fill gaps with genre-consistent choices. If it contradicts itself, follow the more specific statement.
- <memory> covers what happened after <setting> was written, and may already include the earliest turns still shown in <transcript>. A fact in both is one event, not two — never narrate it twice. <profile> is confirmed fact, rewritten whenever it changes, so it is newer than any <episodes> entry; <episodes> state how an event ended; <recall> is a verbatim excerpt that may come from mid-event, often absent — use only when present and it fits the scene. Where <memory> and <transcript> disagree, the transcript is newer and wins.

